---
layout: article
title: Block traffic with Firewall
description: Turn what Appwrite Analytics shows into Firewall rules for a site. Block a country, serve only one country, or deny a bot or a page, starting from the dashboard.
---

Analytics shows who visits your site, and [Firewall](/docs/products/firewall) decides who reaches it. When a property tracks a site hosted on [Appwrite Sites](/docs/products/sites), its dashboard can start a Firewall rule for that site from a country, region, city, hostname, page, or bot on the dashboard.

# Before you start {% #before-you-start %}

- The property must be linked to the site: its domain has to be one of the site's domains. See [Properties](/docs/products/analytics/properties#sites).
- Your role must allow creating Firewall rules in the project.

# Start a rule from the dashboard {% #start %}

{% only_dark %}
![Menu for United States on the Countries tab, with Block this country and Allow only this country](/images/docs/analytics/dark/value-menu.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Menu for United States on the Countries tab, with Block this country and Allow only this country](/images/docs/analytics/value-menu.avif)
{% /only_light %}

1. Open the property's dashboard.
2. Right-click a value in a card, such as a country on the **Countries** tab of **Locations**.
3. Select **Block this country**, **Allow only this country**, or **Create firewall rule**.

Each value becomes a condition on the new rule.

- **Country**: **Block this country** adds a condition that matches the selected country. **Allow only this country** adds one that matches every other country.
- **Region** or **city**: **Create firewall rule** adds a condition that the region or city equals the selected value.
- **Hostname**: **Create firewall rule** adds a condition that the hostname equals the selected value.
- **Page**, **entry page**, or **exit page**: **Create firewall rule** adds a condition that the path equals the page.
- **Bot**: **Create firewall rule** adds a condition that the user agent contains the bot's name, such as `GPTBot`.

**Allow only this country** also adds a condition that the country is known, so requests whose location Appwrite can't resolve still reach your site.

Other values, such as browsers and channels, have no Firewall action.

# Review and create the rule {% #create %}

{% only_dark %}
![Create firewall rule page with a Country equals United States condition, the Deny action, and the estimated impact](/images/docs/analytics/dark/firewall-rule.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create firewall rule page with a Country equals United States condition, the Deny action, and the estimated impact](/images/docs/analytics/firewall-rule.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The **Create firewall rule** page opens for the linked site, with the condition and the **Deny** action filled in.

1. Enter a **Rule name**, such as `Block GPTBot`.
2. For a bot whose dashboard name has a space, such as **ChatGPT User** or **Google AdsBot**, change the condition's value to the spelling in the bot's user agent, such as `ChatGPT-User` or `AdsBot-Google`.
3. Check **Estimated impact**, which shows how many of the site's recent requests the rule would have matched, and their share of its traffic.
4. To verify that a visitor is a person instead of denying every request, select the **Challenge** action. See [Actions](/docs/products/firewall/actions).
5. To apply the rule only on certain days or at certain times, turn on **Schedule**.
6. Click **Create rule**.

The rule applies to the site from then on, and appears on the site's **Firewall** tab. To manage it later, open the site's **Firewall** tab, or open **Firewall** in your project and select the site on the **Sites** tab. See [Rules](/docs/products/firewall/rules).

# Analytics and Firewall numbers {% #numbers %}

The two products count different things, so their numbers don't match. Analytics counts the events your tracking code sends, once per pageview or action. Firewall counts every request to the site, including scripts, styles, and images, and requests from clients that never run your tracking code.

After a rule denies traffic, those visitors no longer load your pages, so they stop appearing in Analytics.
